    [--repo=<site-repo>]  repository's pins (with --repo) and every installed
    [--format=json]       file the engine refuses to load — a shadowed adapter,
                          an ambiguous identity, a case-confusable name, a
                          symlink, a nested or near-miss .json, a reserved name
                          — each as a ROW carrying the engine's own message, its
                          code, its remediation, and which source it is about
                          plus whether it refused that whole source or one
                          adapter. Adapters that are installed and NOT loaded
                          (a plugin bundling a name a reviewed adapter already
                          answers to) print too, and deliberately never change
                          the exit code: that is precedence working, not a
                          fault. This is the one command
                          that reports those states instead of dying on them,
                          which is the point: it is what an operator can still
                          run when the repository is in one. Offline-honest —
                          it never claims a live verdict, and names what it did
                          not check.
                          All three: exit 0 healthy, 1 anything surfaced,
                          2 usage/IO. Every run, passing or failing, ends with
                          the `deferred` list — the checks needing a live
                          target, the manifest-shipped PHP it deliberately does
                          not load, and the cross-manifest guards that belong to
                          a pin set.
  adapter keygen          Mint the CUSTOMER-ORGANIZATION Ed25519 key that
    --out=<secret-file>   certifies this repository's own adapters. The secret
    [--key-id=<id>]       is written to the path you name at mode 0600 and
                          NEVER inside a site repository — a site repo is
                          committed and published, so a key in one is a
                          published key. Prints the key id and the public key;
                          `adapter certify` registers that public key in the
                          repository's own adapters/authorities.json. This is
                          a customer-organization trust root, explicitly not a
                          Duo endorsement: what it signs reads `Site-certified`,
                          never `Platform-certified`.
  adapter certify         Sign one installed site adapter under that key.
    <site-repo>           Registers the public key in adapters/authorities.json
    --name=<n>            if absent, runs the engine's REAL manifest validators
    --secret-key-file=<f> over the adapter, builds a
    [--key-id=<id>]       duo-site-adapter-certification-bundle/v1 whose
    [--reason=<text>]     evidence is exactly {grammar: <validator verdict>,
    [--pin]               exercised: false, reason}, signs
                          adapters/certifications/<n>.json, verifies what it
                          just wrote through the live verifier, and prints the
                          {name,source,digest} pin object. --pin writes or
                          replaces that exact object in site.duo.json.
